// Practice quiz: Response codes with ResponseEntity
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-boot-basics/response-codes-with-responseentity

::o1 created::Which expression returns 201 with a Location header? {
~ResponseEntity.ok(uri)#Not quite. That returns 200 with the URI as the body.
=ResponseEntity.created(uri).body(saved)#Correct. created takes the URI, and body adds the payload.
~ResponseEntity.noContent().build()#Not quite. That returns 204.
~ResponseEntity.status(404).body(uri)#Not quite. That returns 404.
}

::o1 no body::Why does ResponseEntity.noContent() need .build()? {
~build sends the HTTP request#Not quite. Returning the entity is what sends it.
~build makes it 200#Not quite. The status stays 204.
~build is only for tests#Not quite. It is used in production code.
=There is no body call to finish the response#Correct. build completes a response with no body.
}

::o2 validation failure::A request body is invalid because a required field is blank. Which status fits? {
~404#Not quite. That means the resource is missing.
~201#Not quite. Nothing was created.
=400#Correct. The client must fix the input.
~500#Not quite. The server did not fail.
}

::o2 missing resource::A GET asks for a workshop id that does not exist. Which status fits? {
=404#Correct. The resource is absent.
~204#Not quite. That means success with nothing to return, which hides the miss.
~200 with an empty object#Not quite. A success status hides the miss.
~401#Not quite. That is about authentication.
}

::o3 reading expression::What does the client get from ResponseEntity.notFound().build()? {
=Status 404 and no body#Correct. No body was set.
~Status 200 and null#Not quite. The status is 404.
~Status 404 and a generated JSON error from this expression#Not quite. This expression sets no body.
~An exception at compile time#Not quite. It compiles normally.
}

